`SELECT` hands back every row. Real questions have conditions, and those go in `WHERE`:

```sql
SELECT subject FROM tickets WHERE status = 'Open';
```

The quoting rule trips up beginners most: **text values need single quotes, numbers are written bare**. `'Open'`, `'P1'` and `'EMEA'` are text. `WHERE id = 3` and `WHERE total > 1000` use numbers with no quotes. Drop the quotes and the database looks for a column of that name and fails.

Comparisons behave as you expect — `=`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` — and you can compare a column to a literal or to another column. Combine conditions with `AND` (both true) and `OR` (either true), and negate any condition with `NOT`:

```sql
SELECT subject FROM tickets WHERE status = 'Open' AND priority = 'P1';
SELECT id FROM orders WHERE status = 'Paid' OR status = 'Pending';
SELECT subject FROM tickets WHERE NOT status = 'Resolved';
```

One trap to remember: `AND` binds tighter than `OR`. In `status = 'Paid' OR status = 'Pending' AND total > 1000`, the second condition is grouped first, so every Paid order survives no matter its total. Use parentheses to say exactly what you mean:

```sql
SELECT id FROM orders WHERE (status = 'Paid' OR status = 'Pending') AND total > 1000;
```
